#!/bin/sh
# mux-class_lib: the PANE CLASS vocabulary and its ordering. Sourced
# (functions only).
#
# TWO MARKERS SHARE THIS, and they are still two questions:
#
#     @mux-control    who may TYPE into this pane        (the send gate)
#     @mux-attention  whose attention is OWED            (strip, notifications,
#                                                         next-blocked)
#
# WHY A LIB NOW, when the three words were fine open-coded twice: the ORDERING
# is new, and an ordering is a RULE rather than a default. This tree's own
# guidance is to centralise a rule and leave a default alone, and to do it at
# the moment logic appears rather than before; `mux agent class` is that
# moment. Before it there were two copies of a word list with a clean record;
# after it there would have been three copies of something with a direction.
#
# NOT THE GRANTABLE SUBSET. `libexec/mux-agent` tests `agent|hybrid` where it
# decides whether a POLICY may grant a send, and that is a different rule
# living in the right place: `human` is refused there before the policy file
# is read at all, so it is not a shorter spelling of this list.

# mux_class_valid CLASS -> 0 when it is one of the three.
mux_class_valid() {
  case ${1:-} in
  human|agent|hybrid) return 0 ;;
  esac
  return 1
}

# mux_class_rank CLASS -> 0 agent, 1 hybrid, 2 human. Higher is CLOSER TO THE
# HUMAN, and that single direction is what the one-way rule turns on, for both
# markers at once:
#
#   control    human is the most RESTRICTED (refused to every sender)
#   attention  human is the most VISIBLE (it reaches their surfaces)
#
# So "toward the human" means more restricted AND more visible, and a machine
# may always move that way: it only ever removes its own permission and adds
# the human's sight of the pane. The reverse GRANTS something, which is why it
# needs a human (see `mux agent class`).
#
# AN UNKNOWN WORD RANKS LOWEST on purpose, so it can never read as a move
# toward the human. Callers validate first and this is the second line of
# defence, deliberately in the safe direction rather than loud: a rank
# function with an error path would have two answers for one question.
mux_class_rank() {
  case ${1:-} in
  human)  echo 2 ;;
  hybrid) echo 1 ;;
  *)      echo 0 ;;
  esac
}
